.SS Programs and their means
Odin's
.B core:fmt
carries the runtime's type tables, several hundred kilobytes of text a
page-sized program cannot afford.
A program that formats output imports
.B vsys:libfmt
instead (see
.IR print (2)),
and the smallest programs \(em the kernel's own self-test blobs \(em print
nothing formatted at all.
.PP
A compiled program (a VECTRA02 image) gets a stack of sixteen pages, 64
KiB, and a budget of 128 page frames, 512 KiB, for its text, data and
stack together.
A raw blob (VECTRA01) gets one page, 4 KiB, of stack.
.PP
A program's heap, described in
.IR alloc (2),
is built on first use: 64 KiB of anonymous memory from
.IR segalloc ,
doubled by
.I segbrk
when a request does not fit, up to the kernel's bound on one run.
A server that only posts a service and answers 9P need not touch it.
